-- title:
--   Modular equation between the two Hecke legs on j
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $p$, an integer $M \ge 5$ with $p \nmid M$, a field $L$ of characteristic zero that is a $\{p\}$-cyclotomic extension of $\mathbb{Q}$, a primitive $p$-th root of unity $\zeta \in L$, and an intermediate field $K$ of $L \subseteq L((q))$ equal to `laurentBaseChange L (x1FunctionField (M * p))`, the $L$-subextension of $L((q))$ generated by the image under `coeffEmb` of the $\mathbb{Q}$-subfield generated by the integral form ratios for $\Gamma_1(Mp)$. Let $A$ be a discrete valuation domain with fraction field $L$ such that $p$ lies in its maximal ideal and $\zeta$ lies in the image of $A$, with compatible $A$-algebra structures; let $j \in K$ be nonzero with Laurent image `coeffEmb L jq`. Let $\ell$ be a prime, and let $K_\ell$ be the analogous base change of the function field attached to $\Gamma_1(Mp) \cap \Gamma_0(Mp\ell)$, with $j_\ell \in K_\ell$ nonzero and of the same Laurent image. Write $B =$ `chartAlgFin A K j` and $B_\ell =$ `chartAlgFin A K_\ell j_\ell` for the subalgebras of elements integral over $A[j]$, resp. $A[j_\ell]$, and let $\iota_\alpha, \iota_\beta : B \to B_\ell$ be $A$-algebra maps inducing, on Laurent series, the identity and the index-scaling map `qExpand L ℓ` respectively. Let `data : ModularPolynomialData ℓ` supply $\Phi \in \mathbb{Z}[X][Y]$, monic, of degree $\psi(\ell)$, with $\Phi(j(q), \,$`qExpand ℚ ℓ jq`$) = 0$ in $\mathbb{Q}((q))$. Then for every field $\Omega$ and every ring homomorphism $\varphi : B_\ell \to \Omega$, the polynomial obtained from $\Phi$ by evaluating its $\mathbb{Z}[X]$-coefficients at $\varphi(\iota_\alpha \hat{\jmath})$ over $\Omega$, where $\hat{\jmath} =$ `jChartFin A K j` is $j$ regarded in $B$, vanishes at $\varphi(\iota_\beta \hat{\jmath})$.
--
--   This is the classical modular equation of level $\ell$, asserting that at any $\Omega$-valued point of the Hecke roof the $j$-invariant read through the first degeneracy leg and the one read through the second are $\ell$-isogenous. It feeds the argument that the second leg carries crossing points of the special fibre of the two-chart model of $X_1(Mp)$ to crossing points, via the preservation of supersingularity along $\Phi_\ell$-related $j$-values.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_XOneP_eval_fibrePoly_apply_heckePin_jChartFin_eq_zero_of_modularPolynomialData_twoChartModel_x1_mul.lean

